WS73 runtime receive function regression
=======================================

Run `python3 run_ws73_runtime_rx.py --output /tmp/ws73-runtime-rx.json`.
CC defaults to clang; a compiler with ASAN/UBSAN support is required.
The test opens no USB device and installs no driver.
Use `--artifacts /tmp/new-ws73-rx-artifacts` to retain the generated source,
exact positive ELF and mutation sources/ELFs/stderr in a new directory. The
report hashes these bytes. Without this option the report states that the
temporary artifacts were not retained; replay needs a rebuild.

The runner extracts the current production ws73_native_open,
ws73_native_close and ws73_native_rx_complete bodies without changing their
decisions. It compiles the actual wire.c HCC parser, rather than a second
parser. The report binds bodies, source files, fixture, runner and compiler.

Five temporary compiled mutations must be rejected by actual C assertions:
ignored panic, accepted short notification, retired consumer misclassified,
missing stop check before rearm and omitted notification cleanup. Compilation
failure, timeout or sanitizer startup failure cannot satisfy these controls.
The output is invalidated before work begins so a failed rerun cannot retain
an old PASS. Raw detector failures remain failures.

Scenarios cover stopped/broken/already-owned open, both allocation failures,
both initial submission failures, notification lengths 0..7 and exact8,
zero/advisory/unknown/panic words, transfer errors, zero successful bulk,
valid aggregate, invalid later HCC slot before any consumer, retired Native
consumer, consumer failure, stop during consumption, rearm error and late
callback rejection. Advisory Wi-Fi flow/pool notifications never become DLI
data, credit, Ready or fabricated completion in this path.

Dependencies are visible in fixture.c: single-thread memory access macros,
allocation, USB fill/submit/kill call records, programmable consumer and fault
observation. Failures are injected into actual call sites. The USB kill stub
does NOT implement the kernel's reject/use_count/barrier/wait protocol. A call
to that stub is not proof of callback/ref drain. The Native consumer is not
the Host: generation lookup, packet ownership, credit and Session dispatch
need their own tests. The fixture survivor checks object-local side effects,
not real controller isolation. No time bounds or fairness are established.

The runtime driver already has one bulk RX URB and one notification interrupt
URB, started after bootstrap. This test does not add another receiver, alter
RX size/count or justify a performance change. Actual VM/device regressions,
hardware failure RCA and nonmanual recovery remain separate requirements.
